paragraphs.text:
Assume that a total of $Q$ AFDM frames are allocated for pilot
transmission \cite{cai2026}. Let $N$ denote the number of AFDM
chirp subcarriers \cite{huang2026}. Let $P_{qkn}\in\mathbb C$ be
the pilot symbol transmitted by the $k$th device during the $q$th
AFDM frame on the $n$th chirp subcarrier.
We construct a pilot vector as
$\mathbf p_{qk}=[P_{qk0},\ldots,P_{qk,N-1}]^{\mathsf T}
\in\mathbb C^N$, $k=1,\ldots,K$, $q=0,\ldots,Q-1$.
The time-domain transmitted signal is generated through the inverse
discrete affine Fourier transform (IDAFT) applied to $\mathbf p_{qk}$,
mathematically formulated as \cite{ding2026}
\begin{equation}
 s_{qk}[t]=\frac{1}{\sqrt N}\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}
 P_{qkn}e^{j2\pi(c_1t^2+c_2n^2+nt/N)},
 \label{eq:afdm-time-signal}
\end{equation}
where $P_{qkn}$ represents the $n$th symbol of $\mathbf p_{qk}$.
$c_1$ and $c_2$ are AFDM modulation parameters.
The signal in \eqref{eq:afdm-time-signal} can be represented in matrix
form as
\[
 \mathbf s_{qk}=\mathbf F_{\rm A}^{\mathsf H}\mathbf p_{qk},
\]
where
\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \mathbf F_{\rm A}
 =\left[\frac{1}{\sqrt N}e^{-j2\pi(c_2r^2+rt/N+c_1t^2)}
 \right]_{r,t=0}^{N-1}
 \end{gathered}
 \label{eq:daft}
\end{equation}
represents the discrete affine Fourier transform (DAFT) matrix.
Let $\Delta f$ denote the subcarrier spacing.
Let $T_s=1/(N\Delta f)$ denote the sampling interval.

Assume that the chirp-periodic prefix (CPP) duration satisfies
$N_{\rm CPP}T_s\geq\max_{k,\ell}\tau_{k\ell}$.
With the chirp periodicity, the prefix is \cite{bemani2022}
\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 s_{qk}[t]=e^{j2\pi c_1(t^2-\langle t\rangle_N^2)}
 s_{qk}[\langle t\rangle_N],\\
 -N_{\rm CPP}\leq t<0,
 \end{gathered}
 \label{eq:cpp}
\end{equation}
where $\langle x\rangle_N=x-N\lfloor x/N\rfloor$ and
$2Nc_1\in\mathbb N_+$.
Each AFDM frame spans a duration of
$T_{\rm frame}=(N+N_{\rm CPP})T_s$ \cite{huang2026}.

We use an indicator function $\alpha_k$ to model the device
activity as \cite{cai2026}
\[
 \alpha_k=\begin{cases}
 1,&\text{if the $k$th device is active},\\
 0,&\text{otherwise}.
 \end{cases}
\]
Let $\rho$ represent the probability of a device being active,
i.e., $\Pr(\alpha_k=1)=\rho$, where $\rho\ll1$.
Each device maintains its activity state throughout a transmission
block. Let $X_k=\alpha_kH_k$.
Let
\begin{equation}
 A_{qkn}=P_{qkn}e^{jq\beta_k}.
 \label{eq:effective-pilot}
\end{equation}
After removing the CPP and applying the DAFT, the received signal
$\mathbf Y_q\in\mathbb C^{M\times N}$ during the $q$th AFDM
frame is given by \cite{cai2026}
\begin{equation}
 Y_{qmr}=[\mathbf Y_q]_{mr}
 =\sum_{k=1}^K\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}A_{qkn}X_{kmrn}+W_{qmr},
 \label{eq:observation-tensor}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf W_q=[W_{qmr}]_{m,r}\in\mathbb C^{M\times N}$
is an AWGN matrix with the elements i.i.d.\ drawn from
$\CN(0,\sigma^2)$.
Next, we collect the $Q$ received frames into a single matrix as
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf Y=\mathbf A\mathbf X+\mathbf W,
 \label{eq:mmv}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf Y,\mathbf W\in\mathbb C^{Q\times MN}$,
$\mathbf A\in\mathbb C^{Q\times KN}$, and
$\mathbf X\in\mathbb C^{KN\times MN}$.
Define
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 [\mathbf Y]_{q,mN+r}&=Y_{qmr},\\
 [\mathbf W]_{q,mN+r}&=W_{qmr},\\
 [\mathbf A]_{q,(k-1)N+n}&=A_{qkn},\\
 [\mathbf X]_{(k-1)N+n,mN+r}&=X_{kmrn}.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:mmv-indexing}
\end{equation}
Let $\mathbf P\in\mathbb C^{Q\times KN}$ denote the pilot matrix
known at the satellite receiver, with entries
$[\mathbf P]_{q,(k-1)N+n}=P_{qkn}$.
For simplicity, we impose the condition that $\mathbf P$ is partial
orthogonal, specifically requiring that
$\mathbf P\mathbf P^{\mathsf H}=KE_p\I_Q$, where each device
has the same pilot energy budget
$E_p=\|\mathbf p_{qk}\|^2$ \cite{cai2026}.
The design of partial orthogonal $\mathbf P$ is deferred to
Section~\ref{sec:simulation-settings}.

Alternatively, message-passing algorithms provide a low-complexity
iterative approach for approximately calculating the MMSE estimator
\cite{cai2026}. In message passing, we factorize the joint posterior
distribution according to Bayes' theorem and the conditional
independence relationships among the variables, expressed as
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 &q(\mathbf X,\{H_k\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\alpha_k\}_{k=1}^{K}\mid\mathbf Y)\\
 &\quad\propto\prod_{j=1}^{MN}p(\mathbf y_j\mid\mathbf x_j)\\
 &\qquad\times\prod_{k=1}^{K}
 p(X_k\mid H_k,\alpha_k)q_{\rm tr}(H_k)p(\alpha_k),
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:working-posterior}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf x_j\in\mathbb C^{KN}$ and
$\mathbf y_j\in\mathbb C^Q$ denote the $j$-th column of
$\mathbf X=[\mathbf x_1,\ldots,\mathbf x_{MN}]$ and
$\mathbf Y=[\mathbf y_1,\ldots,\mathbf y_{MN}]$, respectively.
Here, $q$ is the working posterior,
$p(\mathbf y_j\mid\mathbf x_j)=\CN(\mathbf y_j;
\widehat{\mathbf A}\mathbf x_j,\widehat\sigma^2\I_Q)$ is the
Gaussian likelihood from \eqref{eq:mmv},
$p(X_k\mid H_k,\alpha_k)$ imposes $X_k=\alpha_kH_k$, and
$p(\alpha_k)=\rho^{\alpha_k}(1-\rho)^{1-\alpha_k}$.
The MMSE channel estimate and posterior probability of activity
can be calculated as
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 X_k^{\rm MMSE}&=\E_q[X_k\mid\mathbf Y],\\
 p_k^{\rm Bayes}&=q(\alpha_k=1\mid\mathbf Y).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:targets}
\end{equation}
